1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,360
A living history lesson ties together the islands of New York City.

2
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:12,880
Each bridge has a unique story to tell.

3
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:19,980
From the East River to the Hudson, beneath the steel and stone,

4
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:27,980
are tales of genius, courage, greed, and death.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,900
Our first story begins with a sea of humanity blanketing the Verrazano Narrows Bridge at the start of the New York City Marathon.

6
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:48,820
Spanning New York's harbor, the bridge, built between 1959 and 1964, has represented a way out for the residents of Staten Island.

7
00:00:48,821 --> 00:01:00,480
For until 1964, Staten Island's only connections to New York City were the ferries that crossed between Staten Island, Brooklyn, and the island of Manhattan.

8
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:08,760
The Verrazano Narrows Bridge gave the rural and isolated islanders their first land link to Brooklyn.

9
00:01:09,340 --> 00:01:10,720
But it was a mixed blessing.

10
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:17,520
Overnight, Staten Island crawled with displaced Brooklynites looking for an affordable place to live.

11
00:01:17,521 --> 00:01:22,240
There was a massive out-migration of not just middle class but working class people,

12
00:01:22,620 --> 00:01:24,200
and that's what the Verrazano Bridge did.

13
00:01:24,560 --> 00:01:28,160
It allowed the Italian working class and lower middle class of Brooklyn

14
00:01:28,161 --> 00:01:30,520
an escape route to the suburbs.

15
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:34,040
That escape route was the Verrazano Bridge, and the suburb was Staten Island.

16
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:39,240
A migration triggered by the chairman of the Triborough Bridge and Tunnel Authority,

17
00:01:39,241 --> 00:01:42,000
the politically powerful Robert Moses,

18
00:01:42,001 --> 00:01:47,500
builder of the 1964 World's Fair, Lincoln Center, and Shea Stadium.

19
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:53,340
To create room for the Verrazano's substantial approach roads,

20
00:01:53,780 --> 00:01:57,420
Moses ousted 8,000 Brooklyn inhabitants from their homes.

21
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:03,020
In the 1930s, 40s, 50s, as roads were being built in New York,

22
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:06,320
and we were building it before there was federal aid for roads,

23
00:02:07,020 --> 00:02:11,220
Bob Moses had the power of eminent domain as the city's construction coordinator.

24
00:02:11,221 --> 00:02:16,060
He can decide that it was in the public interest to take your house, to take your property.

25
00:02:16,780 --> 00:02:20,620
And there's very little that you could do other than go through the political process.

26
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:26,360
He would have design drawings and engineers come out and you would have to move.

27
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:30,100
Nobody would think of ever bucking City Hall or Robert Moses.

28
00:02:30,580 --> 00:02:38,340
Nor did anyone buck Moses when he nixed rapid transit, forcing everyone to travel the Verrazano by car.

29
00:02:38,341 --> 00:02:42,220
You needed a car which said you were from some kind of an economic class.

30
00:02:42,860 --> 00:02:45,400
So in many ways it polarized the city.

31
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:53,280
And so designing a bridge the wrong way to not introduce transit created one community that was more affluent and more white.

32
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:59,180
And that was the community of Staten Island and it left Brooklyn to be more minority, a poorer community.

33
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:07,380
As he'd done before with his bridges, Robert Moses hired the now elderly master bridge builder, Othmar Amon.

34
00:03:08,020 --> 00:03:10,820
Critics charged that Amon may have designed the bridge,

35
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:13,520
but he designed Robert Moses' bridge.

36
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:16,760
Costs spiraled quickly.

37
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:21,240
At completion, the Verrazano cost over $320 million.

38
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:26,420
Workers spent a year building the Verrazano's two towers.

39
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:30,580
Built perpendicular to the Earth's surface,

40
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:34,200
they are 1 and 5 eighths inches farther apart at their tops

41
00:03:34,201 --> 00:03:36,920
than at their bases due to the curvature of the Earth.

42
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:44,380
The 690-foot-tall towers are higher than most of Manhattan's skyscrapers.

43
00:03:44,381 --> 00:03:51,380
From anchorage to anchorage, the structure extends 6,690 feet.

44
00:03:51,381 --> 00:04:00,380
The fantastic Verrazano flaunts a 4,260-foot center span and double-decked six-lane roadway.

45
00:04:00,381 --> 00:04:01,699
six-lane roadway.

46
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:07,440
Amund said the bridge was conceived as an enormous object drawn as faintly

47
00:04:07,441 --> 00:04:11,988
as possible. Ed Fennell's father worked on the cables.

48
00:04:11,989 --> 00:04:15,020
The younger, more crazy iron workers

49
00:04:15,021 --> 00:04:21,860
would take their American Bridge hard hat, it was like a bowl, and the cable was getting weaved like

50
00:04:21,861 --> 00:04:27,420
this, the main cable, and they would sit in their hard hat and slide down the cable to get the lunch.

51
00:04:27,421 --> 00:04:33,700
It took six months for the crew to spin the four main cables that support the roadway,

52
00:04:34,080 --> 00:04:38,060
testing each wire for size, strength, and metallic content.

53
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:44,700
Then the cables were carried from anchorage to anchorage on a wheel that laid them side by side and one above the other.

54
00:04:45,200 --> 00:04:50,820
It's called the Eternal Wheel, and it was hung on a cable over where the main cable would lay,

55
00:04:50,940 --> 00:04:53,680
and it just ran 24 hours a day in a Verrazano bridge.

56
00:04:53,681 --> 00:04:58,940
In a suspension design, the parallel cables are the main support member.

57
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:05,560
The cables are strung over towers that run the length of the bridge and are anchored at each end.

58
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:14,720
These are huge blocks, heavy blocks, and their purpose is to hold the cable, to resist the cable.

59
00:05:15,220 --> 00:05:18,520
And then you have the cable goes to the other end, to the other anchorage.

60
00:05:18,521 --> 00:05:20,800
and it's anchored in the anchorage.

61
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:25,800
Typically, the cables would splay out into bands of wires,

62
00:05:25,900 --> 00:05:28,320
groups of wires that would wrap around eye bars,

63
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:32,900
steel beams that are then embedded in the cable, in the anchorage,

64
00:05:33,160 --> 00:05:35,040
and they're embedded further and further,

65
00:05:35,180 --> 00:05:37,800
and then you get to the very base of the anchorage,

66
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:40,300
and there's a plate. It's attached to a plate.

67
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:44,300
Now, on top of that plate is masonry

68
00:05:44,301 --> 00:05:48,480
and a very heavy block that resists that pull.

69
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:51,580
So you've got the cable wanting to be pulled this way,

70
00:05:51,940 --> 00:05:53,760
and it's trying to lift this block up,

71
00:05:53,860 --> 00:05:55,280
and that's what holds it.

72
00:05:56,800 --> 00:06:00,120
Suspenders hung from the main cables support the deck.

73
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:02,980
It was the longest suspension bridge in the world

74
00:06:02,981 --> 00:06:05,520
and held that record until 1981.

75
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:10,420
Named for the 16th century Italian explorer

76
00:06:10,421 --> 00:06:11,920
who discovered New York Harbor,

77
00:06:11,921 --> 00:06:17,080
the Verrazano Narrows was dedicated on November 21, 1964.

78
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:21,300
When it came time to present Othmar Amun,

79
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:26,180
Robert Moses introduced the 85-year-old as the greatest living bridge engineer,

80
00:06:26,580 --> 00:06:28,320
perhaps the greatest of all time.

81
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,140
Although Amun is credited with many bridges,

82
00:06:31,340 --> 00:06:34,240
the Verrazano Narrows was his consummate achievement.

83
00:06:37,100 --> 00:06:40,900
Coming up, a medieval symbol of New York City.

84
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:50,520
The Verrazano Narrows broke San Francisco's Golden Gate Bridge span record by 60 feet.

85
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:54,920
New York Bridges will return on Modern Marvels.

86
00:06:58,280 --> 00:07:04,960
East of Liberty Island, joining the island of Manhattan to Brooklyn, is the Brooklyn Bridge.

87
00:07:04,961 --> 00:07:18,960
After 13 years of construction, in the spring of 1883, New Yorkers marveled at their new bridge, framed against a sky brilliant with fireworks.

88
00:07:23,220 --> 00:07:32,440
Spanning the East River with its neo-Gothic towers and dense network of cables, the Brooklyn Bridge was the largest suspension bridge in the world.

89
00:07:32,441 --> 00:07:39,440
So impressive, New Yorkers compared it to the pyramids, and it hadn't opened for carriage traffic a minute too soon.

90
00:07:39,441 --> 00:07:42,634
Manhattan had been practically nothing in 1800.

91
00:07:42,635 --> 00:07:46,440
By 1875 there were a million people on Manhattan Island.

92
00:07:46,441 --> 00:07:53,440
By 1910 there were going to be two million people, and they could see it coming, and they were desperate for space.

93
00:07:53,441 --> 00:08:00,440
An East River bridge could solve overcrowding on the island's Lower East Side, and spur development of Brooklyn as well.

94
00:08:00,441 --> 00:08:05,584
well. A joint venture, it was originally called the New York and Brooklyn Bridge.

95
00:08:05,585 --> 00:08:06,600
After the city's

96
00:08:06,601 --> 00:08:10,685
consolidation in 1898, the New York name was dropped.

97
00:08:10,686 --> 00:08:13,460
The Brooklyn Bridge cost an enormous

98
00:08:13,461 --> 00:08:17,603
amount of money to build at the time that it was constructed.

99
00:08:17,604 --> 00:08:19,980
It required the resources of a much

100
00:08:19,981 --> 00:08:25,840
larger city than just New York, the island of Manhattan at the time, or the city of Brooklyn

101
00:08:25,841 --> 00:08:28,220
at the time, and the two of them had to come together.

102
00:08:29,220 --> 00:08:34,640
Before the Brooklyn Bridge, the only way to cross the unpredictable East River was by ferry.

103
00:08:35,560 --> 00:08:38,740
We may think they're romantic today because we don't have to depend on them,

104
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:42,540
but believe me, when that was the only way you could cross the river, you hated them.

105
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:48,600
In 1865, over 41 million people crossed the East River by ferry,

106
00:08:49,420 --> 00:08:52,480
sandwiched on boats moving between Brooklyn and Manhattan.

107
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,900
East River passengers were as likely to lose their lives as make their destination.

108
00:08:57,760 --> 00:09:01,920
One miserable commuter was legendary engineer John Augustus Roebling.

109
00:09:02,580 --> 00:09:05,320
Roebling pushed for a suspension bridge over the East River.

110
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:09,160
As a famous bridge designer and owner of a wire rope company,

111
00:09:09,340 --> 00:09:11,280
Roebling wanted to build the bridge himself.

112
00:09:12,140 --> 00:09:14,660
His proposal was met with mixed reaction

113
00:09:14,661 --> 00:09:18,700
due to the fact that many of the turn-of-the-century bridges collapsed.

114
00:09:18,701 --> 00:09:26,920
Along comes Roebling with a design for an almost 1600 foot span, unthought of, unheard of.

115
00:09:26,921 --> 00:09:30,240
This is the moonshot of the mid 19th century.

116
00:09:30,241 --> 00:09:37,340
What basically made the Brooklyn Bridge possible were improvements in wire technology.

117
00:09:37,341 --> 00:09:42,960
Much in the same way that improvements in elevator technology made it possible to build

118
00:09:42,961 --> 00:09:45,380
the skyscraper.

119
00:09:45,381 --> 00:09:51,340
They were able to come up with a wire that was strong enough to bridge the East River without snapping.

120
00:09:52,440 --> 00:09:58,760
John Augustus Roebling's wire rope cable was the breakthrough in modern suspension bridge technology.

121
00:09:59,620 --> 00:10:06,140
In 1869, the same year the bridge's plan was approved, John Roebling died of tetanus.

122
00:10:06,260 --> 00:10:09,900
He would never see his cobweb of cables reach to the sky.

123
00:10:09,901 --> 00:10:15,640
The entire construction of the Brooklyn Bridge fell to his 32-year-old son, Washington.

124
00:10:16,380 --> 00:10:23,360
One year later, Washington Roebling ordered pneumatic caissons floated onto the East River and sunk into the riverbed.

125
00:10:24,100 --> 00:10:31,860
Inside these wooden subterranean chambers, immigrant labor paid $2.25 a day, mined for bedrock.

126
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:36,660
They needed a firm foundation for the bridge's towers, which would stand in the river.

127
00:10:36,661 --> 00:10:44,300
They would begin to put successive amounts of stone on top of the caissons,

128
00:10:44,680 --> 00:10:47,620
and this weight would push the caisson down.

129
00:10:48,880 --> 00:10:53,000
Compressed air was pumped into the caissons to keep them from sinking into the mud.

130
00:10:53,420 --> 00:10:55,760
As the depth increased, so did the air pressure.

131
00:10:57,480 --> 00:11:02,400
And then they would send the workers in there with shovels and picks and so forth,

132
00:11:02,401 --> 00:11:07,280
and they would literally start excavating all of the material that's in the caisson.

133
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:12,700
Workers entering and leaving through an airlock experienced excruciating pain.

134
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:16,460
The changes in air pressure of the caissons proved deadly.

135
00:11:17,300 --> 00:11:22,460
Caissons disease, also known as the bends, killed three men and paralyzed others,

136
00:11:22,920 --> 00:11:24,440
including Washington Roebling.

137
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:30,220
They thought it had something to do with poor oxygen quality in the caissons.

138
00:11:30,221 --> 00:11:34,960
They thought that the men were in too cramped conditions.

139
00:11:35,640 --> 00:11:41,080
They even looked at the mud to make sure that there wasn't some kind of gaseous exhalation

140
00:11:41,081 --> 00:11:46,140
that the mud was giving off when they were doing the excavation work within the caissons.

141
00:11:46,760 --> 00:11:50,540
Permanently unable to walk or talk from the crippling bends,

142
00:11:51,180 --> 00:11:54,600
Washington Roebling became known as the man in the window.

143
00:11:54,601 --> 00:12:01,600
He supervised the bridge from his townhouse in Brooklyn Heights using Emily, his wife, as an intermediary.

144
00:12:01,601 --> 00:12:06,600
It was dangerous, but it was work. And this was important to the immigrants.

145
00:12:06,601 --> 00:12:08,215
Now they were paid very little.

146
00:12:08,216 --> 00:12:11,600
They went on strike and actually I believe the strike was broken.

147
00:12:11,601 --> 00:12:16,600
Because after all the bridge builders could always go out there and scoop up another bunch of immigrants.

148
00:12:16,601 --> 00:12:23,600
Digging through the riverbed of loose mud, silt and stones, the Brooklyn Foundation hit bedrock at 44 feet.

149
00:12:23,601 --> 00:12:27,600
The foundation on the Manhattan end rested on sand.

150
00:12:27,601 --> 00:12:37,600
A geologist as well as an engineer, Washington Roebling learned from his borings that in Manhattan bedrock was about 175 feet below the riverbed.

151
00:12:37,601 --> 00:12:40,600
He'd reached about 78 feet.

152
00:12:40,601 --> 00:12:42,600
Roebling would not risk more men.

153
00:12:42,601 --> 00:12:51,600
So, after an analysis of the stratum from his borings, he determined that if the particular layer that the tower would be sitting on, which was compressed silt,

154
00:12:51,601 --> 00:12:57,580
compressed silt had not moved in the past 100 million years, most likely it would never move.

155
00:12:58,120 --> 00:13:03,300
Roebling put an end to the excavation, finished construction of the towers, and began to lay the

156
00:13:03,301 --> 00:13:07,922
cables that held up the framework of the bridge.

157
00:13:07,923 --> 00:13:12,640
A rope, a very thin line would be shot across the

158
00:13:12,641 --> 00:13:18,600
East River, or hauled across the East River, and attached to this piece of rope was the first

159
00:13:18,601 --> 00:13:25,562
strand of wire, some of which is actually thinner in diameter than a human hair.

160
00:13:25,563 --> 00:13:26,780
And they would

161
00:13:26,781 --> 00:13:33,520
painstakingly pull the first of the wire strands across, hang them over the top of the towers,

162
00:13:34,060 --> 00:13:40,540
and affix them to the anchorages that sit on either end of the bridge and anchor the cables

163
00:13:40,541 --> 00:13:41,492
to the earth.

164
00:13:41,493 --> 00:13:47,280
The cable was made up of a large number of individual wires which were parallel

165
00:13:47,281 --> 00:13:48,100
to one another.

166
00:13:49,320 --> 00:13:51,040
Neither the cables nor the wires

167
00:13:51,041 --> 00:13:52,680
were twisted in any way.

168
00:13:53,420 --> 00:13:54,900
Suspended from a board seat

169
00:13:54,901 --> 00:13:56,360
with ropes at its corners,

170
00:13:57,080 --> 00:13:59,580
men traveled between the towers on pulleys.

171
00:14:01,780 --> 00:14:03,740
And then when they had a bundle

172
00:14:03,741 --> 00:14:05,100
of a couple of thousand

173
00:14:05,101 --> 00:14:08,000
or a few thousand of those strands,

174
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:10,860
they would have a machine that came along

175
00:14:10,861 --> 00:14:12,660
that was like a large spinner.

176
00:14:13,080 --> 00:14:16,740
And it would basically pull the strands together

177
00:14:16,741 --> 00:14:18,740
and it would bind them.

178
00:14:18,741 --> 00:14:20,740
They'd repeat the process until they had

179
00:14:20,741 --> 00:14:22,740
over a dozen bundles of wire.

180
00:14:22,741 --> 00:14:25,740
Then they would be bound into one large cable,

181
00:14:25,741 --> 00:14:28,740
which are the main cables seen on the bridge today.

182
00:14:28,741 --> 00:14:32,740
It was the longest suspension bridge in the world.

183
00:14:32,741 --> 00:14:35,740
The wires on the bridge, steel wires,

184
00:14:35,741 --> 00:14:39,740
with a coating of zinc, a galvanized coating,

185
00:14:39,741 --> 00:14:41,740
provided tremendous strength

186
00:14:41,741 --> 00:14:45,740
and also provided tremendous resistance to corrosion.

187
00:14:46,740 --> 00:14:51,880
Each of the four steel cables could hold over 11,000 tons.

188
00:14:52,520 --> 00:15:00,240
It was determined in later years that the Brooklyn Bridge was six times stronger than necessary for the time in which it was built.

189
00:15:01,080 --> 00:15:06,820
For everyday New Yorkers, the bridge's strength was every bit as important as its view.

190
00:15:06,821 --> 00:15:16,456
The first great bridge had a promenade that was purposely centered in the middle of the bridge and raised above all the traffic and all of the barriers so that

191
00:15:16,457 --> 00:15:18,820
pedestrians would have the finest view.

192
00:15:20,060 --> 00:15:21,100
And indeed they did.

193
00:15:21,700 --> 00:15:26,600
John Robin, designer of the Brooklyn Bridge, considered it a boulevard in the sky.

194
00:15:27,460 --> 00:15:33,120
From the bridge, New Yorkers could glimpse their future as a world-class metropolis.

195
00:15:33,121 --> 00:15:42,120
Just north of the Brooklyn Bridge and the Manhattan Bridge is the mechanical looking Williamsburg.

196
00:15:42,121 --> 00:15:48,120
Built because for all the hoopla, the Brooklyn Bridge just hadn't gotten the job done.

197
00:15:48,121 --> 00:15:57,120
Ferries were still packed in the 1890s and New Yorkers were frantically lobbying for a second East River Bridge.

198
00:15:57,121 --> 00:16:04,120
It was logical that Manhattan be bridged to Williamsburg because Williamsburg was a bustling

199
00:16:04,121 --> 00:16:09,120
port and home to the super rich owners of the companies based there.

200
00:16:09,121 --> 00:16:12,207
Under pressure, Chief Engineer Leffert L.

201
00:16:12,208 --> 00:16:15,520
Buck and architect Henry Hornbostell erected

202
00:16:15,521 --> 00:16:21,200
a suspension bridge in just seven years, less than half the time it took to build the elder

203
00:16:21,201 --> 00:16:24,840
statesman Brooklyn Bridge.

204
00:16:24,841 --> 00:16:28,080
The bridge goes back with my own history of my parents

205
00:16:28,081 --> 00:16:30,620
who were both immigrants from Europe

206
00:16:30,621 --> 00:16:32,240
who ended up on the Lower East Side.

207
00:16:32,241 --> 00:16:34,160
It's a very typical story.

208
00:16:34,161 --> 00:16:37,300
And they crossed the bridge, the big bridge to Brooklyn,

209
00:16:37,301 --> 00:16:39,800
and then lived in Williamsburg on the other end.

210
00:16:39,801 --> 00:16:42,880
And my mother even told me stories on very hot nights

211
00:16:42,881 --> 00:16:47,000
that they would go out and they would sleep on the bridge.

212
00:16:47,001 --> 00:16:51,440
The steel towered Williamsburg Bridge was begun in 1896

213
00:16:51,441 --> 00:16:54,360
opened with great fanfare in 1903.

214
00:16:55,980 --> 00:16:59,140
With a main span of 1,600 feet,

215
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:02,100
it was the longest suspension bridge in the world,

216
00:17:02,600 --> 00:17:06,440
wrenching from the Brooklyn Bridge a title it held for 20 years.

217
00:17:07,780 --> 00:17:09,980
One of the things that the Williamsburg Bridge did

218
00:17:09,981 --> 00:17:12,900
was give these poor residents of the Lower East Side,

219
00:17:13,020 --> 00:17:15,480
and by the time it opened and really got going, it was the Jews,

220
00:17:15,860 --> 00:17:20,120
it gave them an escape route across the river to Williamsburg,

221
00:17:20,121 --> 00:17:21,500
and they took it.

222
00:17:21,620 --> 00:17:24,860
If you were poor, if you were working on the Lower East Side

223
00:17:24,861 --> 00:17:27,300
and you were living in Williamsburg, which was a common trip,

224
00:17:27,860 --> 00:17:29,240
that's how you got back and forth,

225
00:17:29,300 --> 00:17:31,400
and these bridges enabled you to do that.

226
00:17:31,820 --> 00:17:32,920
They cared for the poor.

227
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:34,720
Once the bridge was up,

228
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:38,240
previous well-to-do citizens of Williamsburg fled.

229
00:17:38,840 --> 00:17:40,780
When the penniless foreigners arrived,

230
00:17:40,920 --> 00:17:43,120
six-story tenements waited to house them.

231
00:17:43,860 --> 00:17:46,800
By 1910, Williamsburg had the densest population

232
00:17:46,801 --> 00:17:51,880
and highest infant mortality rate in all of the newly unified greater New York.

233
00:17:52,680 --> 00:17:55,600
The Williamsburg became known as Jews' Bridge

234
00:17:55,601 --> 00:17:59,380
because Jewish immigrants walked daily between their old neighborhood in Brooklyn

235
00:17:59,381 --> 00:18:01,600
and their new neighborhood in Williamsburg.

236
00:18:02,040 --> 00:18:04,440
And walking was the major mode of transportation.

237
00:18:05,080 --> 00:18:07,840
So they built promenades. This is 16 feet wide.

238
00:18:07,841 --> 00:18:11,080
They had a second one on the other side of the bridge, 16 feet wide,

239
00:18:11,480 --> 00:18:16,540
32 feet devoted to pedestrians, and the pedestrians filled every inch of it.

240
00:18:16,800 --> 00:18:23,720
With its cumbersome lattice construction, the overall effect aesthetically was an ungainly mess.

241
00:18:24,400 --> 00:18:31,740
One unkind critic of the day, John DeWitt Warner, described it as the surrender of the city beautiful to the city vulgar.

242
00:18:31,741 --> 00:18:35,280
This bridge is suspended, held up by just four cables.

243
00:18:35,900 --> 00:18:42,660
Each cable is 19 inches in diameter, has 7,696 parallel pencil-thin wires,

244
00:18:43,040 --> 00:18:46,620
and they spread out into 37 strands in the anchorage.

245
00:18:46,800 --> 00:18:52,140
Those strands, each strand is wrapped around an eye bar which looks like the eye of a needle.

246
00:18:52,720 --> 00:18:59,300
And those eye bars are embedded in the concrete and masonry structure which weighs 130,000 tons.

247
00:18:59,920 --> 00:19:01,284
So it's embedded in that.

248
00:19:01,285 --> 00:19:05,320
At the very bottom there's a steel plate and that's what resists the pull.

249
00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:14,200
The cable is pulling on those eye bars which are then pulling on that plate which is trying to lift up that 130,000 pound structure.

250
00:19:14,201 --> 00:19:17,020
and that's why this bridge stays in place.

251
00:19:17,800 --> 00:19:21,920
But Leffert Buck cut corners with the Williamsburgs cables.

252
00:19:22,460 --> 00:19:25,780
By not using the process of galvanizing the wires,

253
00:19:26,220 --> 00:19:28,720
covering them with a zinc coating to resist corrosion,

254
00:19:29,180 --> 00:19:32,100
he ended up costing New York City a fortune.

255
00:19:33,220 --> 00:19:37,100
They didn't protect that cable and galvanize the wires,

256
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:40,580
so they put just pure, plain steel wires in that cable,

257
00:19:40,581 --> 00:19:43,120
and of course, within a short amount of time,

258
00:19:43,121 --> 00:19:47,440
water got into the cable and began to corrode the cable.

259
00:19:48,160 --> 00:19:50,140
Within three years of its opening,

260
00:19:50,300 --> 00:19:53,180
the Williamsburg Bridge needed strengthening and straightening.

261
00:19:53,840 --> 00:19:57,620
Years later, it was considered so unsafe, it was shut down.

262
00:19:57,980 --> 00:20:00,060
If little else, it did what it set out to do,

263
00:20:00,600 --> 00:20:03,560
relieve the congestion on the Brooklyn Bridge.

264
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:09,840
Next, a Jazz Age addition to the East River.

