319
00:24:48,820 --> 00:24:50,720
Not one application was approved.

320
00:24:51,580 --> 00:24:54,400
When the Queensborough was open to service in 1909,

321
00:24:54,820 --> 00:24:57,940
you were coming out of the cowboy tradition of the West.

322
00:24:58,460 --> 00:25:02,560
It was very popular for people to go over Niagara Falls in a wooden barrel,

323
00:25:02,561 --> 00:25:08,100
and it would be very popular for people to leap off the Queensborough Bridge into the river.

324
00:25:09,520 --> 00:25:15,340
Jumping from the center of the $20 million bridge would have landed the Daredevil on Roosevelt Island,

325
00:25:15,820 --> 00:25:20,300
where bridge designer Gustav Lindenthal's cantilever spans meet.

326
00:25:21,620 --> 00:25:24,900
A suspension bridge is supported from above.

327
00:25:25,400 --> 00:25:28,480
A cantilevered bridge is supported in this case from a tower.

328
00:25:28,481 --> 00:25:35,040
It is supported from the tower and there is a structure that allows it to go out in an L shape.

329
00:25:35,280 --> 00:25:40,040
So you have the tower and then you have a structure above, a truss type structure,

330
00:25:40,280 --> 00:25:45,380
that will allow you that bridge to stand by itself without anything supporting it above

331
00:25:45,381 --> 00:25:47,240
and without anything supporting it below.

332
00:25:47,480 --> 00:25:49,020
So it's cantilevered.

333
00:25:50,300 --> 00:25:57,400
Two anchor arms extend from opposite shores and are joined by a middle span which is supported by piers.

334
00:25:57,401 --> 00:26:01,160
The arms support the midsection by the force of tension.

335
00:26:02,040 --> 00:26:04,180
The piers absorb the downward force.

336
00:26:04,720 --> 00:26:09,180
The trusses are metal triangles that support the floor either above or below.

337
00:26:09,640 --> 00:26:17,360
It was going to be designed to link Manhattan with Blackwell's Island, now known as Roosevelt Island, and Queens.

338
00:26:18,400 --> 00:26:22,320
And essentially they knew it was going to be for trains.

339
00:26:22,321 --> 00:26:27,240
and they knew that the suspension bridge was not the best form because of the fact

340
00:26:27,241 --> 00:26:32,640
that if you had trains moving across the span and they were not in tandem the

341
00:26:32,641 --> 00:26:38,220
bridge would have a tendency to deflect and so the the trusses were considered

342
00:26:38,221 --> 00:26:44,280
much stronger for rail traffic. The scheduled opening of the Queensboro was

343
00:26:44,281 --> 00:26:49,140
delayed when another cantilever span the Quebec Bridge which stretched across the

344
00:26:49,141 --> 00:26:53,692
St. Lawrence River collapsed. The public was horrified.

345
00:26:53,693 --> 00:26:56,340
The Scientific American reported

346
00:26:56,341 --> 00:27:01,680
that dangerous changes had been made to designer Lindenthal's plan and that the Queensborough

347
00:27:01,681 --> 00:27:03,109
Bridge could also fail.

348
00:27:03,110 --> 00:27:07,520
They called in experts to test the bridge's strength and work continued

349
00:27:07,521 --> 00:27:09,408
on the bridge approaches.

350
00:27:09,409 --> 00:27:14,620
The Queensborough Bridge was redesigned and opened on March 30, 1909.

351
00:27:14,621 --> 00:27:24,620
Despite Lindenthal's best efforts, the Queensborough Bridge is clumsy looking and its decorative features are out of place.

352
00:27:24,621 --> 00:27:25,321
Henry F.

353
00:27:25,322 --> 00:27:31,620
Hornbostel, the bridge's consulting architect, reportedly said when he first saw the finished bridge,

354
00:27:31,621 --> 00:27:34,733
My God, it's a blacksmith's shop.

355
00:27:34,734 --> 00:27:41,620
For the citizenry of Queens, the 7,449 foot bridge was a fiscal windfall.

356
00:27:41,621 --> 00:27:42,321
windfall.

357
00:27:42,322 --> 00:27:47,700
When they built the Queensborough Bridge, it was built literally into a rural

358
00:27:47,701 --> 00:27:48,401
county.

359
00:27:48,402 --> 00:27:54,400
There's a famous photograph of the Queensborough Bridge being built, taken from

360
00:27:54,401 --> 00:28:00,340
Long Island City, a rural Long Island City, and you're literally watching the city and

361
00:28:00,341 --> 00:28:06,132
the future coming to Queens County. Real estate development spread fast.

362
00:28:06,133 --> 00:28:07,660
Farms were leveled,

363
00:28:07,661 --> 00:28:08,644
Roads were paved.

364
00:28:08,645 --> 00:28:13,680
Trolley lines from the Queen's side of the bridge were laid to previously remote areas.

365
00:28:14,240 --> 00:28:20,460
Queen's population of 275,000 ran up to over 1 million residents by the 1930s.

366
00:28:21,440 --> 00:28:27,220
Today, the bridge formerly known as the Queensborough is called the 59th Street Bridge by locals,

