00:28:53,500 --> 00:28:57,400
The George Washington Bridge is the most heavily trafficked

374
00:28:57,401 --> 00:28:59,320
of all New York City's bridges,

375
00:28:59,880 --> 00:29:03,220
carrying 95.8 million cars a year.

376
00:29:03,221 --> 00:29:07,120
New York Bridges will continue on Modern Marvels.

377
00:29:10,480 --> 00:29:19,340
It was a technological tour de force, this new bridge that crossed the Hudson River from northern Manhattan to Fort Lee, New Jersey.

378
00:29:20,300 --> 00:29:24,900
Begun in 1927 and dedicated in October of 1931,

379
00:29:24,901 --> 00:29:26,440
President Franklin D.

380
00:29:26,441 --> 00:29:31,060
Roosevelt called the 59 million dollar George Washington Bridge

381
00:29:31,061 --> 00:29:34,540
almost superhuman in perfection.

382
00:29:36,340 --> 00:29:40,640
What Amund did with this bridge that made it so significant in engineering terms

383
00:29:40,641 --> 00:29:43,980
is he applied a new theory called the deflection theory.

384
00:29:44,460 --> 00:29:49,700
Now basically what the deflection theory said was that when you get a bridge of a certain length

385
00:29:49,701 --> 00:29:59,300
Its size is so big that the bridge itself can steady itself from the effects of moving traffic and wind.

386
00:29:59,740 --> 00:30:06,160
Ammon reasoned that the stiffening trusses used to check the flexibility of earlier suspension bridges like the Manhattan

387
00:30:06,161 --> 00:30:10,980
that were designed for railroad traffic were not necessary on the GWB.

388
00:30:11,760 --> 00:30:17,940
The dead weight of the bridge deck along with the four great cables would be ample to withstand heavy wind.

389
00:30:17,941 --> 00:30:23,120
after the bridges steel towers were sunken firmly into rock and concrete

390
00:30:23,121 --> 00:30:26,160
the rope cables were strung from the anchorages

391
00:30:26,161 --> 00:30:30,480
two spinning wheels one at the anchorage site in Fort Lee New Jersey

392
00:30:30,481 --> 00:30:32,720
the other at the anchorage site in Manhattan

393
00:30:32,721 --> 00:30:35,860
traveled back and forth creating strands

394
00:30:35,861 --> 00:30:39,940
then spun into one of four cables three feet in diameter

395
00:30:39,941 --> 00:30:44,940
a mile long strong enough to resist the pull of a thousand locomotives

396
00:30:44,941 --> 00:30:53,600
At completion, the GWB spanned almost twice the distance of its 19th century East River rival, the Brooklyn Bridge,

397
00:30:54,120 --> 00:30:57,000
making it the longest suspension bridge in the world,

398
00:30:57,420 --> 00:31:01,600
and another career achievement for its chief engineer, Othmar Amon.

399
00:31:02,140 --> 00:31:05,960
It almost doubled the length of any previous bridge in terms of its span.

400
00:31:06,580 --> 00:31:13,220
It's 3,500 feet long between the towers, which is the equivalent of 15 city blocks.

401
00:31:13,221 --> 00:31:17,840
It's like Madison Avenue between 42nd Street and 57th Street,

402
00:31:18,220 --> 00:31:22,660
elevated six stories above the ground, twice as wide,

403
00:31:23,100 --> 00:31:24,660
and thronged with buses and cars.

404
00:31:25,180 --> 00:31:28,260
A great admirer of Roebling's Brooklyn Bridge,

405
00:31:28,840 --> 00:31:32,740
Amann originally planned to jacket the George Washington in stone.

406
00:31:33,880 --> 00:31:38,780
The 604-foot towers would house an observation deck in restaurants,

407
00:31:38,781 --> 00:31:48,060
But shortage of money forced him to abandon that idea in favor of the square-edged, 335-foot silver-colored towers we see today.

408
00:31:49,020 --> 00:31:57,520
Millions of white-hot rivets driven into place by pneumatic hammers hold the more than 43,000 tons of steel together.

409
00:31:59,040 --> 00:32:06,400
In the beginning of the design, he felt that some masonry in the form of concrete would be necessary from an engineering standpoint.

410
00:32:06,401 --> 00:32:13,400
So the idea was that the steel towers would be sufficient to hold up the structure during construction,

411
00:32:13,401 --> 00:32:22,400
but that concrete would have to be poured on the outside layers of the steel to provide enough strength to actually support the traffic once the bridge was open.

412
00:32:22,401 --> 00:32:27,400
As they developed in the engineering, they discovered that even the concrete would not be necessary.

413
00:32:27,401 --> 00:32:35,400
The artistic appeal of the exposed latticework, while accidental, became the most popular

414
00:32:35,401 --> 00:32:40,180
feature of the George Washington Bridge.

415
00:32:40,181 --> 00:32:46,080
After a public contest held by the Port Authority to choose a name for the bridge, George Washington

416
00:32:46,081 --> 00:32:48,040
