440
00:34:53,101 --> 00:34:55,740
that had already been contributed by the city.

441
00:34:56,420 --> 00:35:00,960
Robert Moses, who was a great proponent of automobile traffic,

442
00:35:00,961 --> 00:35:12,160
recognized the need for another bridge to go from northern Manhattan to Wards Island and then into Queens.

443
00:35:12,840 --> 00:35:15,480
He wanted to increase access to Long Island,

444
00:35:15,481 --> 00:35:22,920
where he had been installing a number of parks and beaches and recreation areas of this kind.

445
00:35:23,620 --> 00:35:29,480
Both single-family homes and apartment buildings were demolished to make way for the bridge approach ramps.

446
00:35:29,481 --> 00:35:33,580
they were given either a month or two notice to get out of their homes.

447
00:35:35,160 --> 00:35:39,760
And nobody, these were solid working class people who had invested in their homes.

448
00:35:39,840 --> 00:35:44,500
It was probably the largest investment of their life and they were thrown out in a matter of seconds.

449
00:35:45,060 --> 00:35:48,920
Given that Moses built bridges without factoring in public mass transit

450
00:35:48,921 --> 00:35:51,040
and only the middle class owned cars,

451
00:35:51,041 --> 00:35:56,320
people accused Moses of racism or being just plain anti-poor people.

452
00:35:57,060 --> 00:36:00,860
But Moses had a way of giving to the poor as well as taking away from them.

453
00:36:01,620 --> 00:36:04,880
So much concrete was needed to pave the triborough's roadways

454
00:36:04,881 --> 00:36:09,460
that factories reopened and 5,000 unemployed men went back to work.

455
00:36:10,060 --> 00:36:13,900
High above the bridge, little had changed since the days of the Brooklyn Bridge.

456
00:36:14,580 --> 00:36:18,040
With the triborough's main span more than 100 yards high,

457
00:36:18,260 --> 00:36:23,720
painters had to raise themselves up to the top by scaffolds they built using ropes and pulleys.

458
00:36:23,721 --> 00:36:30,200
If a man didn't fall to his death off the scaffold, he could still die of lead poisoning from the paint.

459
00:36:32,020 --> 00:36:37,500
Triborough's main span is a half-mile-long suspension bridge arching the well-named Hellgate,

460
00:36:38,040 --> 00:36:41,900
the body of water between Wards Island and Astoria in Queens.

461
00:36:43,080 --> 00:36:48,600
Within its anchorages are cables containing enough wire to travel twice around the world.

462
00:36:48,601 --> 00:36:53,860
Robert Caro, author of The Power Broker, a book about Robert Moses,

463
00:36:54,280 --> 00:36:58,800
described the Triborough as not a bridge so much as a traffic machine.

464
00:37:04,060 --> 00:37:09,220
With all the historical significance collectively held by the bridges of New York City,

465
00:37:09,221 --> 00:37:16,020
it's a surprise that 56% of the bridges operated by the city need extensive reconstruction.

466
00:37:16,021 --> 00:37:21,020
Over the years, the East River bridges in particular were ignored,

467
00:37:21,021 --> 00:37:24,020
not by the residents who marveled at their design,

468
00:37:24,021 --> 00:37:29,020
not by commuters dependent upon a landling to get them from here to there,

469
00:37:29,021 --> 00:37:31,020
but by the city of New York.

470
00:37:31,021 --> 00:37:36,020
In 80 years, these bridges to the past were barely maintained.

471
00:37:36,021 --> 00:37:39,020
The steel is still rusting on the Manhattan Bridge,

472
00:37:39,021 --> 00:37:43,020
and it has torsional or twisting problems.

473
00:37:43,021 --> 00:37:48,860
Its designer put its subway tracks on the outer edges of the bridge instead of the middle.

474
00:37:49,480 --> 00:37:55,180
When a loaded train comes across the Manhattan, the bridge twists toward the side on which the train is running.

475
00:37:56,000 --> 00:38:00,460
The bridge has been strengthened after a closing, but the problem continues.

476
00:38:01,880 --> 00:38:09,080
Sam Schwartz inherited that design flaw on the Manhattan as well as serious corrosion problems in the Williamsburg

477
00:38:09,081 --> 00:38:14,740
after he was appointed chief engineer of the Department of Transportation for the city of New York.

478
00:38:15,380 --> 00:38:19,060
I inherited the bridge, and I had seen all these calculations,

479
00:38:20,220 --> 00:38:26,680
and I called in a number of scientists who, reading these calculations, said,

480
00:38:26,681 --> 00:38:28,370
you have a real problem.

481
00:38:28,371 --> 00:38:33,440
This bridge may not survive, and this is 1986, 1987, another five years,

482
00:38:33,800 --> 00:38:37,980
and there is even a probability that the bridge may not make it another day.

483
00:38:38,940 --> 00:38:43,880
And so I went over scenarios of what happens when a suspension bridge fails,

484
00:38:44,480 --> 00:38:46,020
and it is not a happy outcome.

485
00:38:46,120 --> 00:38:47,300
It's not as if there's warning.

486
00:38:48,140 --> 00:38:52,680
A suspension bridge had failed just 20 years earlier over the Ohio River,

487
00:38:52,681 --> 00:38:55,000
the Silver Bridge over the Ohio River,

488
00:38:55,200 --> 00:38:59,600
and the description of that failure was that there was a momentary shake,

489
00:39:00,160 --> 00:39:05,360
and then it fell right to the river with about 45 people on it who all perished.

490
00:39:05,361 --> 00:39:13,880
As early as 1920, engineers noticed cable wires beginning to break and corrosion in various wires on the Williamsburg Bridge.

491
00:39:14,160 --> 00:39:19,680
They applied linseed oil and fish oil to stop it and then decided to wrap the wire with zinc.

492
00:39:20,080 --> 00:39:27,380
But the zinc next to the steel resulted in an electric potential that created more corrosion instead of alleviating the problem.

493
00:39:27,381 --> 00:39:34,560
This is absolutely a crisis and when I first witnessed this in 1988, I found situations

494
00:39:34,561 --> 00:39:36,420
that were even worse than this.

495
00:39:36,421 --> 00:39:41,420
I found a column like this that was split right down the middle and there were many

496
00:39:41,421 --> 00:39:47,780
others in which were paper-thin steel and as a result we had to close all traffic from

497
00:39:47,781 --> 00:39:48,780
the bridge.

498
00:39:48,781 --> 00:39:53,780
We had to stop the trains, stop the cars, the trucks and even the pedestrians from walking

499
00:39:53,781 --> 00:39:57,780
across the bridge until we made some temporary repairs.

500
00:39:57,781 --> 00:40:05,780
The problem here is this cable over here, these vertical cables, along with the other

501
00:40:05,781 --> 00:40:08,507
suspender cables, they cradle the truss.

502
00:40:08,508 --> 00:40:11,780
So they're attached to each other at the bottom.

503
00:40:11,781 --> 00:40:18,780
That means if one major cable snaps or has a problem, both cables are useless.

504
00:40:18,781 --> 00:40:23,640
The scariest moment I had was in trying to learn what is the disaster mode,

505
00:40:23,720 --> 00:40:26,180
what happens if a cable would break.

506
00:40:26,840 --> 00:40:29,380
And if a cable would break, the second cable would break,

507
00:40:29,620 --> 00:40:32,200
that means this side of the bridge would have no support.

508
00:40:32,800 --> 00:40:34,980
Effectively, the roadway would tilt,

509
00:40:35,580 --> 00:40:38,360
and everything on it obviously would plummet it to the East River.

510
00:40:41,660 --> 00:40:45,240
In the redesign for the bridge, that's being corrected.

511
00:40:45,241 --> 00:40:49,160
So these suspender cables will be attached to the truss,

512
00:40:49,161 --> 00:40:53,980
independent of those suspender cables which will also be anchored into the truss.

513
00:40:53,981 --> 00:40:57,320
Currently all the East River bridges share funds

514
00:40:57,321 --> 00:41:00,680
under a multi-billion dollar Federal Bridge Replacement Act.

515
00:41:00,681 --> 00:41:05,480
When a bridge gets to this point and the corrosion is so pervasive,

516
00:41:06,240 --> 00:41:08,420
you cannot save this bridge.

517
00:41:09,040 --> 00:41:13,400
It's unthinkable that I could just take pieces of steel, I'm not Superman,

518
00:41:13,401 --> 00:41:19,120
and I could just tear pieces of steel, shake them loose, and take them off the bridge.

519
00:41:19,660 --> 00:41:21,920
This can't be saved. It has to be replaced.

520
00:41:22,300 --> 00:41:27,720
The Williamsburg Bridge, like the others, never had its expansion joints cleaned or lubricated,

521
00:41:28,020 --> 00:41:29,860
and so they've corroded and frozen.

522
00:41:30,440 --> 00:41:33,300
The structure should expand and contract with the temperature.

523
00:41:33,820 --> 00:41:36,260
Instead, it's pulling itself apart.

524
00:41:36,880 --> 00:41:39,720
Reclamation of these bridges falls to the iron workers.

525
00:41:39,721 --> 00:41:44,120
The worst of it, the hardhats will tell you, is the fear of lead poisoning.

526
00:41:44,620 --> 00:41:51,180
Long-term exposure to the lead causes kidney disease, hypertension, stroke, nerve damage, and mental impairment.

527
00:41:52,160 --> 00:41:56,440
The sheer physical demand, the hazards associated with lead exposure,

528
00:41:57,080 --> 00:42:04,780
the prospect of heavy objects swinging into you or falling onto you under very changeable and difficult conditions,

529
00:42:05,080 --> 00:42:08,780
I think requires a level of just simple bravery.

530
00:42:08,781 --> 00:42:13,180
Using a pneumatic drill they call the hell dog to bust old rivets.

531
00:42:13,181 --> 00:42:16,180
Workers grind and scrape away at the old paint.

532
00:42:16,181 --> 00:42:20,780
Workers have tracked lead-filled dust into their own homes, infecting their families.

533
00:42:20,781 --> 00:42:24,780
And what about the communities that live at the base of these bridges?

534
00:42:24,781 --> 00:42:27,780
Historically, they are the poorest citizens in New York.

535
00:42:27,781 --> 00:42:38,780
No one has yet done the study to look at lead that has really come from deterioration of bridge structure.

536
00:42:38,781 --> 00:42:40,840
to see if that gets into the community

537
00:42:40,841 --> 00:42:43,880
and whether that is causing elevated lead levels

538
00:42:43,881 --> 00:42:45,380
among especially children.

539
00:42:45,520 --> 00:42:49,420
Because it's children who play in soil, in dust,

540
00:42:49,500 --> 00:42:51,120
and who put things in their mouths

541
00:42:51,121 --> 00:42:53,000
and with contaminated hands and toys

542
00:42:53,001 --> 00:42:55,540
have an opportunity to swallow lead

543
00:42:55,541 --> 00:42:56,400
rather than breathe it.

544
00:42:56,401 --> 00:42:59,840
The commonly accepted wisdom

545
00:42:59,841 --> 00:43:03,400
that one paint chip the size of your little fingernail,

546
00:43:03,900 --> 00:43:04,980
my little fingernail,

547
00:43:05,180 --> 00:43:08,680
would be enough to render a young child severely toxic.

548
00:43:09,760 --> 00:43:15,920
Fortunately, problems of toxicity are being addressed as work continues on the East River spans.

549
00:43:16,760 --> 00:43:21,740
The bridges of New York City are bridges to our past as well as our future.

550
00:43:22,400 --> 00:43:26,680
As the 19th century architect Montgomery Shiler said in 1883,

551
00:43:27,500 --> 00:43:30,640
the work which is likely to be our most durable monument

552
00:43:30,641 --> 00:43:34,580
and to carry some knowledge of us to the most remote posterity

553
00:43:34,581 --> 00:43:37,080
is a work of bare utility.

554
00:43:37,520 --> 00:43:42,600
Not a shrine, not a fortress, not a palace, but a bridge.

